      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) in a Cartesian plane, we need to use the distance formula. The distance \\(d\\) between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nFirst, identifying the coordinates of the given points:\n- \\((x_1, y_1) = (-3, -4)\\)\n- \\((x_2, y_2) = (4, -5)\\)\n\nNext, we substitute the given points into the distance formula:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(4 - (-3))^2 + (-5 - (-4))^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the expressions inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{4 + 3)^2 + (-5 + 4)^2}\n\\]\n\nWhich becomes:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(4 + 3)^2 + (-5 + 4)^2} \n                            \\]\n\nThe final simplification of the exponents gives us:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{7^2 + (-1)^2}\n                          \\]\n\nCalculating the squares:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{49 + 1}\n                           \n                         \\]\n\nSo, adding these results:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{50}\n\\]\n\nExpressing \\(\\sqrt{50}\\) in simplest radical form:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2}\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of square roots \\(\\sqrt{a \\times b} = \\sqrt{a} \\times \\sqrt{b}\\):\n\n\\[\nd = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]",
